WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.840
Let&amp;#39;s start with the direct pollutants.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.970
Lead used to be added to gasoline to improve combustion

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.690
in automotive engines.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:11.530
It reduced preignition of the air fuel mixture called knock.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.540
However, lead is a potent neurotoxin.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.080
Lead emissions from gasoline reduce the IQ of children growing up

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.650
in polluted cities.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.430
This was no minor effect.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.950
Lead levels were high enough in the 1970s

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:29.020
to reduce the average IQ of Americans who grew up then by five points.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.150
Lead pollution has been greatly reduced due to stringent regulations that

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.400
banned its use in gasoline and restricted

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.740
use elsewhere across the economy.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.440
The average lead levels in Americans&amp;#39; blood

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.200
has gone down by more than a factor of 10.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.420
This is one of the many examples where environmental regulations have greatly

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.090
improved human welfare and have had a positive economic benefit that

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.870
greatly exceeded the direct cost of imposing the regulation.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.930
Next, nitrogen oxides-- our atmosphere is mostly

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.300
diatomic nitrogen, which is inert.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.180
NOx and other forms of reactive nitrogen are

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.670
formed by breaking the nitrogen bonds, as can happen in a combustion process

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.740
where oxygen reacts with atmospheric nitrogen

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.420
at high temperatures and pressures.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.390
NOx emissions can be reduced by altering the combustion process

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.760
or by cleaning up the gases afterwards to remove the NOx.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.530
Modern cars do both.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:28.410
They subtly adjust the combustion inside the piston to reduce NOx formation.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.950
And they use a catalytic converter to change NOx back

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.540
into regular diatomic nitrogen.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.480
Environmental regulations have driven the widespread adoption

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.370
of NOx reducing technology, which has succeeded

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:45.010
in reducing total NOx emissions, even as energy use has gone up.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.640
But the success is less dramatic than for lead,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.170
perhaps because there are an enormous number

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.990
of different kinds of NOx emission sources, and the cost of controls

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.710
is relatively high.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:00.630
The US has reduced overall NOx emissions by about 40% since 1980.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.200
Sulfur oxides differ from lead and NOx in that

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.900
they come from sulfur contained in the fossil fuels as they are produced.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.760
Coal, gas, and oil all contain significant amounts of sulfur

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.760
when they&amp;#39;re extracted from the ground, where significant

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.600
can mean about 1% or more sulfur by weight.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.870
The sulfur contained in these fuels is admitted

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.630
to the atmosphere as sulfur oxides, SOx, when the fuels are burned.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:28.000
SOx converts to sulfuric acid in the atmosphere,

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
with obvious negative consequences for human health,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.120
for buildings, and for natural ecosystems damaged by acidic rain.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.170
Sulfur emissions can be controlled by removing it from the fuel

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:42.830
before it&amp;#39;s sent to the consumer and/or by using cleanup technology,

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.470
often called a scrubber.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.860
Scrubbers for removing sulfur typically work by using a base like calcium

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.690
and then making a waste product like calcium sulfate, which in some cases

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.020
is sold as a wallboard in a clever piece of industrial ecology.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.960
In the United States, typical SOx concentrations

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.560
have been reduced by roughly a factor of five since 1980.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.480
Other rich industrial economies have also achieved deep reductions.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.200
Even China now has strong enough emissions

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.030
controls that their sulfur emissions have

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.670
begun to decline in the last few years, even while energy use has skyrocketed.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.060
The difference between sources of NOx, mostly nitrogen from the air and SOx,

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.110
sulfur from the fuel, has important implications for control.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.710
If sulfur is removed from fuels, for example at the refinery,

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:34.260
one can be sure that the final users of those fuels, drivers of cars or trucks,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.310
will not be emitting any sulfur.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.110
One cannot say the same about NOx, as the amount of NOx emitted depends

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:44.090
on details of the control technology in the individual vehicle.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.180
For example, on how well a car&amp;#39;s catalytic converter is working.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.400
Even a hydrogen combustion engine would still

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.460
produce NOx, even though the fuel has no nitrogen or carbon.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.200
The fact that control of NOx depends on the operation

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.350
of a myriad of post-combustion control devices

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:03.140
makes regulation and enforcement harder.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.870
The final direct pollutant, carbon monoxide,

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.560
is a product of incomplete combustion.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.810
It is made when fuels are burned without quite enough oxygen. As with NOx,

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.610
its production can be reduced by altering the combustion process,

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:22.170
and it can be reduced by catalytic converters located after the combustion

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:27.130
source that oxidize carbon monoxide back to carbon dioxide.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.570
Since 1980, the US has reduced carbon monoxide concentrations

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.200
by roughly a factor of six.

